/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Interpolation Interpolation
 * @ingroup DspCore
 * @brief Float/double linear, bilinear and trilinear interpolation arithmetic.
 *
 * Include `<DspCore_Interpolation.h>` or
 * `<DspCore/Interpolation/Arithmetic.h>`. No operation allocates, throws,
 * or owns/modifies table storage.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Interpolation_Formulas Arithmetic
 *
 * NormalizedLinear(value0,value1,t) evaluates a line with normalized t;
 * NormalizedBilinear and NormalizedTrilinear compose it on independent
 * normalized axes. Values outside [0,1] extrapolate.
 * No boundary policy is hidden in the formulas. Use consistent float or double
 * arguments (or an explicit template argument); integer-only arithmetic is
 * rejected at compile time. Double computations are never reduced to float.
 *
 * The normalized linear formula is
 * @f[
 * L(v_0,v_1,t)=(1-t)v_0+tv_1.
 * @f]
 * With x-fast corners, the higher-dimensional forms compose that operator:
 * @f[
 * B(f_{00},f_{10},f_{01},f_{11},t_x,t_y)=
 * L\bigl(L(f_{00},f_{10},t_x),L(f_{01},f_{11},t_x),t_y\bigr),
 * @f]
 * @f[
 * T(\mathbf f,t_x,t_y,t_z)=
 * L\bigl(B(f_{000},f_{100},f_{010},f_{110},t_x,t_y),
 *         B(f_{001},f_{101},f_{011},f_{111},t_x,t_y),t_z\bigr).
 * @f]
 *
 * Corner order is x-fast, matching LUT storage:
 * - Bilinear: f00, f10, f01, f11, x, y.
 * - Trilinear: f000, f100, f010, f110, f001, f101, f011, f111, x, y, z.
 *
 * `f(x,y) = L(L(f00,f10,x), L(f01,f11,x), y)`
 *
 * Linear returns exact endpoints at t=0 and t=1 and uses a weighted form
 * for opposite-sign endpoints to avoid overflowing their difference during
 * interpolation. NaN/Inf samples may propagate; extrapolation may overflow.
 * Results are not clipped. Exact endpoints select the corresponding sample
 * even if an unused sample is non-finite.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Interpolation_Coordinates Physical coordinates
 *
 * LinearAt/BilinearAt/TrilinearAt accept physical coordinates and normalize
 * each axis before applying the corresponding normalized formula. Endpoint
 * names use 0/1 rather than min/max because a descending axis is valid:
 * x1 may be smaller than x0. A collapsed axis (x0==x1) produces normalized
 * coordinate zero, selecting its 0-side without dividing by zero.
 * Extrapolation is permitted. Non-finite arithmetic follows normal floating
 * point propagation; these mathematical functions do not return a bool.
 * For a non-collapsed physical axis, its normalized coordinate is
 * @f[
 * t_x=\frac{x-x_0}{x_1-x_0}.
 * @f]
 * When @f$x_0=x_1@f$, DspCore deliberately defines @f$t_x=0@f$; this selects
 * the 0-side and makes descending or degenerate axes explicit rather than
 * treating their endpoint names as min/max.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * // Physical x=15 over x0=10, x1=20, with values 2 and 8: value=5.
 * double value = DspCore::Interpolation::LinearAt(10.,20.,2.,8.,15.);
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_Interpolation_Examples Examples
 *
 * Interpolation/Linear demonstrates physical coordinates, extrapolation and
 * invalid spans. Shared Arduino/desktop code prints actual and expected values
 * and PASS/FAIL.
 *
 * See SPEC-DOM-1 in @ref DspCore_Numeric.
 */
